      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is fundamentally rooted in the non-commutativity of the position ($\\hat{x}$) and momentum ($\\hat{p}$) operators, which is mathematically defined by their commutator $[\\hat{x}, \\hat{p}] = i\\hbar$. Starting from the Robertson-Schrödinger inequality, the general form $\\Delta A \\Delta B \\geq \\frac{1}{2}|\\langle [\\hat{A}, \\hat{B}] \\rangle|$ directly yields $\\Delta x \\Delta p \\geq \\hbar/2$ when substituting the specific commutator for these observables. This derivation reveals that the uncertainty is not a statistical artifact of experimental noise but an intrinsic property of the quantum state itself, arising because a state cannot be a simultaneous eigenstate of both non-commuting operators. Conceptually, this manifests through wave-particle duality: a particle with a perfectly defined momentum corresponds to a plane wave extending infinitely in space ($\\Delta x \\to \\infty$), while a particle localized to a single point requires a superposition of all possible momentum waves ($\\Delta p \\to \\infty$). The Fourier transform relationship between",
      "source": "target",
